Q: Using CSS linear-gradient in SVG A: <p>The issue here is that <code>background-image</code> and SVG's <code>fill</code> are doing two different things.</p>
<p>For an SVG shape like <code>&lt;rect&gt;</code>, I'd normally use an SVG gradient:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;svg width=&quot;100&quot; height=&quot;100&quot; viewBox=&quot;0 0 100 100&quot;&gt;
  &lt;defs&gt;
    &lt;linearGradient id=&quot;fade&quot; x1=&quot;0%&quot; y1=&quot;0%&quot; x2=&quot;100%&quot; y2=&quot;0%&quot;&gt;
      &lt;stop offset=&quot;0%&quot; stop-color=&quot;transparent&quot;/&gt;
      &lt;stop offset=&quot;100%&quot; stop-color=&quot;black&quot;/&gt;
    &lt;/linearGradient&gt;
  &lt;/defs&gt;

  &lt;rect width=&quot;100&quot; height=&quot;100&quot; fill=&quot;url(#fade)&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/svg&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>So the important part is that the gradient is used as the <code>fill</code> of the rectangle.</p>
<p>If you specifically need CSS <code>linear-gradient()</code>, I'd put the SVG inside an HTML element and apply the background to that element instead:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;gra&quot;&gt;
  &lt;svg width=&quot;100&quot; height=&quot;100&quot;&gt;
    &lt;rect width=&quot;100%&quot; height=&quot;100%&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot;/&gt;
  &lt;/svg&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>.gra {
  width: 100px;
  height: 100px;
  background: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, black);
}
</code></pre>
<p>In that case the CSS gradient belongs to the HTML container, while the SVG itself stays transparent.</p>
<p>So yes, your observation is basically correct: applying a CSS background to the outer <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> can work, but that doesn't mean the same approach will behave like an SVG <code>fill</code> on a graphics element such as <code>&lt;rect&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>For an actual SVG shape, I'd stick with <code>&lt;linearGradient&gt;</code> + <code>fill</code>.</p>

Q: How to print results from the model in the view with CodeIgniter A: <p>To display the data in the view, you need to pass the model method's return value as an associative array element to the view loader within the controller method. The key of associative array elements become the variable name in the view file.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>function uploadData()
{
    $data['anyName'] = $this-&gt;csvToJson-&gt;uploadData();
    $this-&gt;load-&gt;view('uploadCsvForm', $data);
}
</code></pre>
<p>In the view, you can <code>echo $anyName;</code>.</p>

Q: beehiiv api integration with custom form A: <p>Answering this in case its still useful. Move the call to a route handler. In Next.js App Router, <code>app/api/subscribe/route.js</code>:</p>
<pre><code>export async function POST(request) {
  const { email } = await request.json();

  const res = await fetch(
    `https://api.beehiiv.com/v2/publications/${process.env.BEEHIIV_PUB_ID}/subscriptions`,
    {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        Authorization: `Bearer ${process.env.BEEHIIV_API_KEY}`,
      },
      body: JSON.stringify({ email, reactivate_existing: false }),
    }
  );

  const data = await res.json();
  return Response.json(data, { status: res.status });
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then point the component at your own route (same origin, so no CORS, and the key stays server-side in <code>.env.local</code>):</p>
<pre><code>const response = await fetch('/api/subscribe', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
  body: JSON.stringify({ email }),
});
</code></pre>
